Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: a shared fence opens in another process (cranvr)

DXMT's CreateFence stored the name of a shared fence's event in the sync object's private data with
D3DKMT_ESCAPE_UPDATE_RESOURCE_WINE. Wine 11.10 runs that escape for resources only, so it failed with
STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER. DXMT's check of the result was the wrong way round (if (!D3DKMTEscape(...))), so the fence
went on without its name. OpenSharedFence read the name with D3DKMTQueryResourceInfoFromNtHandle, which Wine answers
for resources only, and it read no data even then: Wine's query gives the size alone. No shared fence opened in
another process (cranvr issue #161).

- The fence's NT handle now shares a bundle of three objects, as Wine shares a texture with a keyed mutex
  (D3DKMTShareObjects with a resource, a keyed mutex and the sync object). The resource carries the event's name
  and a tag in its private data. Nobody uses the keyed mutex: Wine shares a sync object with a resource only in such
  a bundle. The escape is gone.
- OpenSharedFence queries the resource of the bundle, opens it with D3DKMTOpenResourceFromNtHandle, which gives the
  name and the sync object, and looks the name up in winemetal's broker (patch 0020). A handle whose data has another
  size or no tag is no fence's: a texture's handle does not open as a fence. The opened fence keeps the bundle, so it
  can share it again.
- Each process logs the open of a fence of another process and its first wait on such a fence on the CPU
  (SetEventOnCompletion) and on the GPU (ID3D11DeviceContext4::Wait), with its process ID. A run then tells which
  process waits on a fence that another process signals.
- util_d3dkmt.h declares D3DKMTCreateKeyedMutex2.

